content:
Every configurable element in your design must be documented with its Shopify settings_schema type. Developers build the settings_schema.json based on what designers specify — missing annotations mean missing merchant controls.

SETTING TYPE REFERENCE:
image_picker — merchant selects/uploads an image. Use for: logo, hero image, background image, collection image.
text — merchant types a single line. Use for: headlines, button labels, SEO alt text. Never use for prices.
richtext — merchant types formatted text (bold, italic, links). Use for: section descriptions, product highlights.
textarea — plain multiline text. Use for: plain copy blocks without formatting.
number — merchant enters a number. Use for: grid columns (1–4), image height (px), product count.
range — slider input. Use for: font size (10–64), opacity (0–100), spacing.
checkbox — boolean toggle. Use for: enable/disable any feature (show price, enable reviews, show vendor).
select — dropdown from fixed options. Use for: page width (narrow/normal/wide), layout (grid/list), aspect ratio (square/portrait/landscape).
color — colour picker. Use for: text colour, background colour, button colour, icon colour.
url — URL input. Use for: CTA link destinations, banner link, external URL.
collection — merchant selects a collection. Use for: featured collection picker, related collection.
product — merchant selects a product. Use for: featured product, hero product.
blog — merchant selects a blog. Use for: featured articles section.
link_list — merchant selects a navigation menu. Use for: header nav, footer nav, mega menu links.
video_url — YouTube or Vimeo URL. Use for: video background, embedded video section.
font_picker — merchant selects from Shopify font library. Use for: heading font, body font, accent font.
metafield — references a metafield definition. Use for: dynamic product specs, badges, custom fields.

ANNOTATION FORMAT (add to Figma component description or sticky note):
@setting [type] '[id]' (default: [value])

EXAMPLE — Hero Section:
@setting image_picker 'image' — hero background image
@setting text 'heading' (default: 'Welcome to our store')
@setting richtext 'subheading'
@setting text 'button_label' (default: 'Shop now')
@setting url 'button_link'
@setting select 'overlay_style' (options: 'none', 'dark', 'light') (default: 'dark')
@setting range 'overlay_opacity' (min: 0, max: 100, step: 5) (default: 40)
@setting color 'text_color' (default: #ffffff)
@setting select 'text_alignment' (options: 'left', 'center', 'right') (default: 'center')
@setting select 'height' (options: 'small', 'medium', 'large', 'full') (default: 'large')

EXAMPLE — Product Card Block:
@setting checkbox 'show_price' (default: true)
@setting checkbox 'show_vendor' (default: false)
@setting checkbox 'show_reviews' (default: true)
@setting checkbox 'enable_quick_add' (default: true)
@setting select 'image_aspect_ratio' (options: 'square', 'portrait', 'landscape') (default: 'square')

MERCHANT CONFIG VS HARDCODED DECISION GUIDE:
Always merchant-configurable: Logo, brand colours, fonts, text copy, image selections, collection/product references, menu assignments, enable/disable feature toggles.
Always hardcoded: Breakpoints (750px, 990px), touch targets (44px min), z-index layers, WCAG contrast minimums, section/block limits, animation easing curves, structural HTML semantics.
Case-by-case: Spacing (use tokens when possible; expose as setting only if critical to layout), animation speed (expose as 'Reduced motion' toggle for accessibility), grid columns (expose if merchant should control it).

FIGMA IMPLICATION: For each section frame, attach a 'Settings Schema' sticky note listing every @setting annotation. Use Figma's component Description field for block settings. Group settings logically (Content settings → Style settings → Layout settings → Behavior settings) — this is the order developers place them in the schema, and it's the order merchants see them in Theme Editor.
